WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Electricity runs everything in an HVAC system. If you don't understand it, you
can't diagnose anything.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
In this lab, you will use a digital multimeter to measure AC voltage (at the
disconnect, contactor, and transformer), DC voltage (at the control board),
resistance (across motor windings and capacitors), continuity (through switches

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Continuity: A complete electrical path. Your meter beeps when continuity exists
(near-zero ohms). No beep = open circuit (broken wire, blown fuse, open switch).

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Capacitance: The ability to store electrical charge, measured in microfarads
(µF). A capacitor must be within ±6% of its rated value to work properly.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Common (C) Terminal: On a compressor, the terminal connected to both the start
and run windings. Used as a reference point for winding resistance measurements.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Every service call requires meter readings.
Measuring voltage confirms power. Measuring amps confirms motor health.
Measuring capacitance confirms capacitor condition. These three measurements

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
